outputs.exact_statement:
Fix a compact set $\mathcal K\subset\Omega$. For two of the preceding finite-volume systems differing by a negative interaction, let $J^{\mathrm{diff}}_{ij}$ be cellwise suprema of its absolute value. If the linear interpolation stays within the same attractive mass bound, then $$\label{eq:pressure-interpolation}
 |\log\Xi(1)-\log\Xi(0)|
 \le \frac{\beta C_{\mathcal K}}2\sum_{i,j}J^{\mathrm{diff}}_{ij}
 \qquad ((\beta,h)\in\mathcal K).$$ In particular, for $$\varphi=\psi_t-t\sum_{j\ge1}\sigma_jw_{R_j},
 \qquad
 \varphi_n=\psi_t-t\sum_{j\le n}\sigma_jw_{R_j},$$ there is a range-independent constant $C'_{\mathcal K}$ such that $$\label{eq:tail-continuity}
 \|Q_\varphi-Q_{\varphi_n}\|_{\mathcal K}
 \le C'_{\mathcal K}\sum_{j>n}\sigma_j .$$

Let $$\varphi=\psi_t-t\sum_{j=1}^k\sigma_jw_{R_j}$$ have finitely many attractive summands, with the empty sum allowed. Fix $\sigma\ge0$ such that $\sum_{j=1}^k\sigma_j+\sigma\le A+1$. Then $$\label{eq:kac-limit}
 Q_{\varphi-t\sigma w_R}\longrightarrow T_\sigma Q_\varphi
 \quad\text{as }R\to\infty$$ uniformly on compact subsets of $\Omega$.

Uniformly on compact subsets of $\Omega$, $$\label{eq:free-pressure-limit}
 q_t(\beta,h)\longrightarrow
 q_*(h):=\max(0,ph,4ph-9p).$$

Let $Q$ be finite and convex on $\Omega$, and let $$H(\beta,d)=\sup_{h\in\mathbb R}\{hd-Q(\beta,h)\}.$$ Fix $d\ge0$ for which $H(\,\cdot\,,d)$ is finite and concave. If $(\beta_0,z)\in\Omega$ satisfies $$H(\beta_0,d)=zd-Q(\beta_0,z),$$ then $$\label{eq:joint-support-identity}
 \{e\in\mathbb R:(e,d)\in\partial Q(\beta_0,z)\}
       =-\partial_\beta^+H(\beta_0,d).$$

Let $t$ satisfy <a href="#eq:t-choice" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:t-choice">[eq:t-choice]</a>, let $Q=Q_\varphi$ for a potential in <a href="#eq:physical-class" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:physical-class">[eq:physical-class]</a>, and let $0<a\le A+1$. Put $H=H_Q$ and $F=T_aQ$. Suppose that $d\ge0$ attains the supremum defining $F(\beta,h)$ at $(\beta,h)\in\Omega$, and put $z=h+\beta ad$. Then, for every $d'\ge0$, $$\label{eq:quadratic-density-support}
 H(\beta,d')\ge H(\beta,d)+z(d'-d)
                         +\frac{\beta a}{2}(d'-d)^2.$$ The finite concave function $H(\,\cdot\,,d)$ has a supergradient at $\beta$. For every $e\in-\partial_\beta^+H(\beta,d)$, $$\label{eq:transform-support-transfer}
 (e,d)\in\partial Q(\beta,z),\qquad
 \left(e+\frac{ad^2}{2},d\right)\in\partial F(\beta,h).$$ If $F$ is differentiable at $(\beta,h)$, the second vector equals $\nabla F(\beta,h)$.

For all sufficiently large $t$, there are $\lambda_1\in B(\lambda_*,r_0/16)$ and nonempty compact convex sets $K_i^1\subset\overline B(v_i,\tau/2)$, $i=1,2,3$, such that $$\label{eq:initial-triple}
 g\in\partial P_1^t(\lambda_1)
       =\mathop{\mathrm{conv}}\bigl(K_1^1\cup K_2^1\cup K_3^1\bigr).$$ All vectors in the $K_i^1$ have nonnegative density coordinate. In particular $\partial P_1^t(\lambda_1)$ contains $\overline B(g,\gamma)$.

Let $u:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R\cup\{+\infty\}$ be proper, lower semicontinuous, and convex, with a domain having nonempty interior. Then $$u(d)=\sup_{h\in\mathbb R}\{hd-u^*(h)\},\qquad
 u^*(h)=\sup_{d\in\mathbb R}\{hd-u(d)\}.$$ At a finite point $d$, equality $u(d)+u^*(h)=hd$ holds precisely when $u(d')\ge u(d)+h(d'-d)$ for every $d'$. In particular, if $K\subset\mathbb R^2$ is nonempty compact convex with a density projection of positive length, then for every $x\in\mathbb R$, $$\max\{xs:(s,d)\in K\}
 =\inf_{z\in\mathbb R}\{h_K(x,z)-zd\},$$ where the maximum is $-\infty$ outside that projection.
